shop on g2  is entirely nullified and the game is  more or less equal  so let's see how jan manages to navigate  this battle  over the break of b5 first and foremost  yan  just moves the queen out of the way now  b5 of course would be a massive blunder  because  knight e5 is the nasty idea that jan has  of course we can move the rook out of  the way but don't go  too quickly here this queen is actually  trapped along with the fork  so b5 no longer an option and that's why  we see the move c5 by alex sanko  still fighting for the center just  without playing b5 just yet  yan follows up now with knight to c3 and  again  b5 is just a little bit premature this  time  knight e5 could still be played uh and  the idea that we had of trading off  this um powerful bishop is is no longer  going to be viable  the best that black can do here is  essentially just to sacrifice in  exchange  and of course that's not exactly what  black is looking for so we still can't  play b5 and that's when we get the move  bishop  e7 by alexianca and this move to my eyes  seems a little bit soft so if you've  been paying attention  the problem with playing b5 has  generally been this  nasty move knight e5 and that's why the  main line here is sort of  aimed at preventing knight e5 from  stopping your idea  of b5 that move of course is queen to c7  now all of a sudden black is controlling  the b7 square  facilitating a bishop b7 idea and is  controlling e5  a little bit more thoroughly so after  the main move  of castles or even d4 we're gonna see  black break out with b5 now  and in this variation we usually see  bishop f4  and simply queen to a7 always  maintaining a grip on the b7 square so  that when knight e5 comes  we simply play bishop b7 and here black  is  pretty much fully equalized and so it's  kind of a mystery to me how alex sanko  plays this bishop e7 move  which is just a slightly more difficult  way  of trying to get this move b5 in we see  castles now by jan napomnishi  and castles by alexenko yan follows up  with this move  d4 and this is where i'm going to say  uh the the opening goes from um  kind of bad to to really even worse  after c  takes d4 black is kind of abandoning any  hope of equalizing here  because now the move b5 is essentially a  pipe dream  we get knight takes d4 and now b5 is  going to be  very very difficult to achieve with  number one this active knight in the  center  number two this bishop bearing down this  now  fully open diagonal and number three uh  the the open d  file nc file are all in white's favor  um so what should um alexenko have done  instead  rather than c takes d4 i'll let you guys  at home try and take a moment to figure  it out here  um black to move here and find an  interesting idea  to try and stay at least in the game  uh okay hopefully you guys came up with  a nice move b5 i think this is the best  chance for black to stay in the game if  we do run into this nasty move knight e5  that i've been talking about  we're simply going to capture this with  black and if bishop takes a it gets  played  c takes d4 is actually a surprisingly  large  amount of compensation for black this  knight is going to have to  leave the c3 square and now all of a  sudden this bishop is a little bit  misplaced  black is rolling through the center and  black  is actually going to be better in this  position now white of course doesn't  have to play knight e5 and there is the  slightly weird looking move knight g5  going for some threats to the king's  side but now after rook a7  black is getting exactly what he wants  in the form of bishop to b7  and is definitely still in this game not  losing  yet so alexenko showing  um that perhaps he didn't calculate  through all these complic  complications quite accurately because  after c takes d4  black really has no hope of equalizing  out of the opening and is going to be in  for a very long and difficult battle in  just a  a great catalan for yawn so we see  knight takes d4  and all the arrows appear back on the  board showing how great white's position  is  uh we see queen c7 by alexenko but it's  a little bit too little too late now  b5 is just not going to happen rook d1  is yan's move rook d8 contesting the d  file  and now bishop e3 and once again you  really see  how far away we are from playing b5  because this bishop  again is attacking a8 so rather than  play a move  like rook a7 or rook b8 both of these  moves just  aren't going to be fast enough white is  just going to take over the center of  the board in the meantime  by the way don't forget about knight c6  you really see  these knights in this bishop  working together just absolutely  eviscerate black's entire position  um so rather than even attempt to go for  b5 in this manner  obviously it's not going to work alex  sanko plays knight b6 instead  hoping for some kind of blockade on d5  rook a to c1  and now alexenko unfortunately has to  more or less has to play this next move  which is just a sign of things going  very very wrong  so in this position i think what  alexander might have been hoping for  is some chance to stick a knight onto  the d5 square  follow that up with b5 and then bishop  b7 and achieve  equality in this manner but after rook a  to c1 we see alexanko sort of give up on  this plan  by playing this move e5 well at first  glance  it looks like this might be a good idea  to activate the remaining light squared  bishop  and in reality it's simply too weakening  on the light squares  black is never going to get this d5  blockade that he was hoping for  and after knight f5 you simply have to  give up this bishop otherwise this  knight is just  way too powerful on f5 and now that the  bishop is gone  we'll see that these light squared  weaknesses around the black king  are what ended up winning the game for  nepo uh the game continues here with  knight c4 so the knights do still have  some life in the position  but after bishop g5 white again is  lookin